WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.025
Unit 3 is exclusions, and the traps.

2
00:00:03.025 --> 00:00:07.307
When a rule in block breaks an
application, Microsoft's advice in Learn

3
00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:11.662
is that an exclusion is better than
turning the rule off or back to audit.

4
00:00:11.662 --> 00:00:16.720
Per-rule exclusions go in the same policy,
under A S R only per rule exclusions,

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.091
as a file path rather than
a folder wherever you can,

6
00:00:20.091 --> 00:00:24.025
and each one goes in the register
with an owner and an expiry.

7
00:00:24.025 --> 00:00:27.257
Learn adds that if Disable
Local Admin Merge is true,

8
00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:29.587
local per-rule exclusions don't apply.

9
00:00:29.587 --> 00:00:33.694
Rollback, when you need it,
moves a rule one stage back:

10
00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:35.987
block to warn, or warn to audit.

11
00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:37.225
Then the dangers.

12
00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:41.245
Configure a rule in Intune or
in Group Policy, never both:

13
00:00:41.245 --> 00:00:46.663
Learn says Group Policy wins by default,
so the Intune value silently loses.

14
00:00:46.663 --> 00:00:50.780
And on Windows Server, Learn lists
a known issue, with no fix date,

15
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.263
where a rule shows compliant
without being enforced.

16
00:00:54.263 --> 00:00:58.009
So on servers, trust the
device-side check and the events,

17
00:00:58.009 --> 00:00:59.860
not the compliance report.
